WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
A pointer that is safe for immediate use may be unsafe when saved for later.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:13.080
Callbacks, asynchronous jobs, threads, event handlers, and containers of pointers all extend the reasoning window.

3
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:20.380
The reviewer must ask not only whether the pointer is valid now, but whether it will still be valid when the stored access is eventually used.

4
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.100
Captured this pointers deserve special attention.

5
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.920
A member function may schedule work that runs after the object has been destroyed.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.340
A callback registry may store a raw pointer without knowing when the target unregisters.

7
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.920
A background task may keep a borrowed pointer from a request that has already completed.

8
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:46.740
These designs need explicit lifetime policy: ownership handoff, cancellation, weak observation, or a guarantee that the owner outlives the work.

9
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:52.940
Shared ownership can keep an object alive for asynchronous work, but it is not always the right answer.

10
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.120
It may extend lifetime longer than intended or create cycles.

11
00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.340
Weak observation can be better when work should simply stop if the target no longer exists.

12
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.520
In either case, lifetime safety is not thread safety.

13
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:14.300
A pointer or smart pointer can ensure an object exists while still leaving its internal state unsafe for concurrent mutation.

14
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.780
Review async pointer use together with synchronization.

15
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.320
What is captured?

16
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.900
Who owns it while work is pending?

17
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.200
What happens during shutdown or cancellation?

18
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.380
What protects mutable state?

19
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:38.600
Tests should cover delayed execution, unregister order, shutdown paths, and cancellation under load because those timings often decide whether a stored pointer remains valid when used.

20
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.620
Clear answers prevent stored pointers from becoming stale access hidden behind event-driven code.
